{"name":"Cliff Swallow","hex":"#ecddd4","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"cliff swallow","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of various birds in the genus Petrochelidon that nest in cliffs.\n Synonym of American cliff swallow (“Petrochelidon pyrrhonota”).","Synonym of American cliff swallow (“Petrochelidon pyrrhonota”)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cliff_swallow"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cliff swallow","blocks":[{"text":"The cliff swallow or  American cliff swallow is a member of the passerine bird family Hirundinidae, the swallows and martins. The generic name Petrochelidon is derived from the Ancient Greek petros meaning \"stone\" and khelidon (χελιδών) \"swallow\", and the specific name pyrrhonota comes from purrhos meaning \"flame-coloured\" and -notos \"-backed\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/Petrochelidon_pyrrhonota_-flight_-Palo_Alto_Baylands-8.jpg/330px-Petrochelidon_pyrrhonota_-flight_-Palo_Alto_Baylands-8.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cliff_swallow"},{"title":"Petrochelidon","blocks":[{"text":"Petrochelidon is a genus of birds known as cliff-nesting swallows."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Cliff_Swallow_Builder.jpg/330px-Cliff_Swallow_Builder.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Petrochelidon"},{"title":"Cliff","blocks":[{"text":"In geography and geology, a cliff or bluff or rock face is an area of rock which has a general angle defined by the vertical, or nearly vertical. Cliffs are formed by the processes of weathering and erosion, with the effect of gravity. Cliffs are common on coasts, in mountainous areas, escarpments and along rivers. Cliffs are usually composed of rock that is resistant to weathering and erosion. The sedimentary rocks that are most likely to form cliffs include sandstone, limestone, chalk, and dolomite. Igneous rocks such as granite and basalt also often form cliffs."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/White_Cliffs_of_Dover_02.JPG/330px-White_Cliffs_of_Dover_02.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cliff"},{"title":"Swallow","blocks":[{"text":"The swallows, martins, and saw-wings, or Hirundinidae, are a family of passerine songbirds found around the world on all continents, including occasionally in Antarctica. Highly adapted to aerial feeding, they have a distinctive appearance. The term \"swallow\" is used as the common name for Hirundo rustica in the United Kingdom and Ireland. Around 90 species of Hirundinidae are known, divided into 21 genera, with the greatest diversity found in Africa, which is also thought to be where they evolved as hole-nesters. They also occur on a number of oceanic islands. A number of European and North American species are long-distance migrants; by contrast, the West and South African swallows are nonmigratory."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Pied-winged_swallow_%28Hirundo_leucosoma%29.jpg/330px-Pied-winged_swallow_%28Hirundo_leucosoma%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Swallow"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Cliff_swallow_7427.jpg/500px-Cliff_swallow_7427.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Cliff_swallow_7427.jpg/1280px-Cliff_swallow_7427.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Cliff_swallow_7427.jpg/500px-Cliff_swallow_7427.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Juvenile cliff swallow","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACliff_swallow_7427.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Cliff_swallow_%2815372086670%29.jpg/500px-Cliff_swallow_%2815372086670%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Cliff_swallow_%2815372086670%29.jpg/1280px-Cliff_swallow_%2815372086670%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Cliff_swallow_%2815372086670%29.jpg/500px-Cliff_swallow_%2815372086670%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Juvenile cliff swallow in a gourd-shaped mud nest","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACliff_swallow_(15372086670).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Petrochelidon_pyrrhonota_1894.jpg/500px-Petrochelidon_pyrrhonota_1894.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Petrochelidon_pyrrhonota_1894.jpg/1280px-Petrochelidon_pyrrhonota_1894.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Petrochelidon_pyrrhonota_1894.jpg/500px-Petrochelidon_pyrrhonota_1894.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Petrochelidon pyrrhonota 1894","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APetrochelidon_pyrrhonota_1894.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Petrochelidon_pyrrhonota_-California%2C_USA-20May2006.jpg/500px-Petrochelidon_pyrrhonota_-California%2C_USA-20May2006.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Petrochelidon_pyrrhonota_-California%2C_USA-20May2006.jpg/1280px-Pet